1
00:00:00,439 --> 00:00:03,680
part 2 chapter 5.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:05,719
Sim had vanished.

3
00:00:05,719 --> 00:00:11,658
A morning came, and he was missing from work: a few thoughtless people commented on his absence.

4
00:00:11,658 --> 00:00:14,359
On the next day nobody mentioned him.

5
00:00:14,359 --> 00:00:18,079
On the third day Winston went into the vestibule of the Records Department to

6
00:00:18,079 --> 00:00:19,939
look at the notice board.

7
00:00:19,939 --> 00:00:22,939
One of the notices carried a printed list of the members of the Chess

8
00:00:22,939 --> 00:00:28,398
Committee, of whom Sim had been won It looked almost exactly as it had looked before—

9
00:00:28,398 --> 00:00:32,299
—nothing had been crossed out— —but it was one name shorter.

10
00:00:32,299 --> 00:00:38,898
It was enough Sim had ceased to exist: he had never existed.

11
00:00:38,898 --> 00:00:40,879
The weather was baking hot.

12
00:00:40,879 --> 00:00:44,779
In the labyrinthine Ministry the windowless, air-conditioned rooms kept to

13
00:00:44,779 --> 00:00:48,859
their normal temperature, but outside the pavement scorched one's feet into the

14
00:00:48,859 --> 00:00:51,680
stench of the Tubes that the rush hours was a horror.

15
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:55,879
The preparations for Hate Week were in full swing, and the staffs of all the

16
00:00:55,879 --> 00:00:58,158
Ministries were working overtime.

17
00:00:58,158 --> 00:01:03,699
Processions, meetings, military parades, lectures wax Works displays, film shows

18
00:01:03,699 --> 00:01:09,139
tele-screen programs all had to be organized stands had to be erected, effigies built,

19
00:01:09,139 --> 00:01:15,620
slogans coined, songs written rumors circulated, photographs faked.

20
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:18,859
Julia's unit in the Fiction Department had been taken off the production of

21
00:01:18,859 --> 00:01:22,520
novels and was rushing out a series of atrocity pamphlets.

22
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:26,658
Winston, in addition to his regular work, spent long periods every day in going

23
00:01:26,658 --> 00:01:30,738
through the back files of the Times and altering and embellishing news items

24
00:01:30,738 --> 00:01:33,139
which were to be quoted in speeches.

25
00:01:33,139 --> 00:01:37,879
Late at night, when crowds of rowdy polls roamed the streets, the town had a

26
00:01:37,879 --> 00:01:42,559
curiously febrile heir The rocket bombs crashed oftener than ever, and sometimes

27
00:01:42,559 --> 00:01:46,219
in the far distance there were enormous explosions which no one could explain

28
00:01:46,219 --> 00:01:51,738
and about which there were wild rumors The new tune which was to be the theme

29
00:01:51,738 --> 00:01:56,478
song of Hate Week (the Hate Song, it was called) had already been composed and was

30
00:01:56,478 --> 00:01:59,359
being endlessly plugged on the telescreens.

31
00:01:59,359 --> 00:02:03,439
It had a savage, barking rhythm which could not exactly be called music, but

32
00:02:03,439 --> 00:02:07,818
resembled the beating of a drum rolled out by hundreds of voices to the

33
00:02:07,818 --> 00:02:10,939
of marching feet, it was terrifying.

34
00:02:10,939 --> 00:02:14,959
The pros had taken a fancy to it, and in the midnight streets it competed with

35
00:02:14,959 --> 00:02:18,260
the still popular It was only a hopeless fancy'.

36
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:22,279
The Parsons children played it at all hours of the night and day, unbearably, on

37
00:02:22,279 --> 00:02:23,978
a comb and a piece of toilet paper.

38
00:02:23,978 --> 00:02:26,600
Winston's evenings were fuller than ever.

39
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:30,318
Squads of volunteers organized by Parsons, were preparing the street for

40
00:02:30,318 --> 00:02:34,639
Hate Week, stitching banners, painting posters, erecting flagstaffs on the roofs

41
00:02:34,639 --> 00:02:38,478
and perilously slinging wires across the street for the reception of streamers.

42
00:02:38,478 --> 00:02:42,620
Parsons boasted the Victory Mansions alone would display 400 meters of

43
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:47,299
bunting. He was in his native element and as happy as a lark. The heat and the

44
00:02:47,299 --> 00:02:50,719
manual work had even given him a pretext for reverting to shorts and an open

45
00:02:50,719 --> 00:02:55,459
shirt in the evenings. He was everywhere at once, pushing, pulling, sawing, hammering,

46
00:02:55,459 --> 00:03:00,079
improvising, jollying everyone along with comradly exhortations and giving out

47
00:03:00,079 --> 00:03:06,579
from every fold of his body what seemed an inexhaustible supply of acrid smelling sweat foreign

48
00:03:06,579 --> 00:03:09,500
ER had suddenly appeared all over London.

49
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:13,639
It had no caption, and represented simply the monstrous figure of Eurasian soldier,

50
00:03:13,639 --> 00:03:17,719
three or four meters high, striding forward with expressionless Mongolian

51
00:03:17,719 --> 00:03:22,699
face and enormous boots, a submachine gun pointed from his hip.

52
00:03:22,699 --> 00:03:26,420
From whatever angle you looked at the poster, the muzzle of the gun, magnified

53
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:30,318
by the foreshortening, seemed to be pointed straight at you.

54
00:03:30,318 --> 00:03:33,799
The thing had been plastered on every blank space on every wall, even

55
00:03:33,799 --> 00:03:36,620
outnumbering the portraits of Big Brother.

56
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:40,699
The pros normally apathetic about the war, were being lashed into one of their

57
00:03:40,699 --> 00:03:44,359
periodical frenzies of patriotism. As though to harmonize with the general

58
00:03:44,359 --> 00:03:48,620
mood, the rocket bombs had been killing larger numbers of people than usual.

59
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:54,559
One fell on a crowded film theater in Stepney, burying several hundred victims among the ruins.

60
00:03:54,559 --> 00:03:58,158
The whole population of the neighborhood turned out for a long, trailing funeral

61
00:03:58,158 --> 00:04:03,080
which went on for hours and was in effect an indignation meeting.

62
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:06,618
Another bomb fell on a piece of waste ground which was used as a playground,

63
00:04:06,618 --> 00:04:09,798
and several dozen children were blown to pieces.

64
00:04:09,798 --> 00:04:14,419
There were further angry demonstrations, Goldstein was burned in effigy, hundreds

65
00:04:14,419 --> 00:04:17,719
of copies of the poster of the Eurasian soldier were torn down and added to the

66
00:04:17,719 --> 00:04:21,438
flames, and a number of shops were looted in the turmoil;

67
00:04:21,438 --> 00:04:25,159
then a rumor flew around that spies were directing the rocket bombs by means of

68
00:04:25,159 --> 00:04:28,879
wireless waves, and an old couple who were suspected of being a foreign

69
00:04:28,879 --> 00:04:32,900
extraction had their house set on fire and perished of suffocation.

70
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:36,740
In the room over Mr cherrington's shop, when they could get there, Julia and

71
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:41,061
Winston lay side by side on a stripped bed under the open Windows naked for the

72
00:04:41,061 --> 00:04:42,618
sake of coolness.

73
00:04:42,618 --> 00:04:47,359
The rat had never come back, but the bugs had multiplied hideously in the heat.

74
00:04:47,359 --> 00:04:49,219
It did not seem to matter.

75
00:04:49,219 --> 00:04:52,278
Dirty or clean, the room was paradise.

76
00:04:52,278 --> 00:04:55,278
As soon as they arrived they would sprinkle everything with pepper bought

77
00:04:55,278 --> 00:04:58,938
on the black market, tear off their clothes and make love with sweating

78
00:04:58,938 --> 00:05:03,199
bodies, then fall asleep and wake to find that the bugs had rallied and were

79
00:05:03,199 --> 00:05:11,359
massing for the counter-attack Four, five, six— —seven times they met during the month of June.

80
00:05:11,359 --> 00:05:15,259
Winston had dropped his habit of drinking gin at all hours. He seemed to

81
00:05:15,259 --> 00:05:17,061
have lost the need for it.

82
00:05:17,061 --> 00:05:22,278
He had grown fatter, his varicus also had subsided, leaving only a brown stain on

83
00:05:22,278 --> 00:05:26,659
the skin above his ankle, his fits of coughing in the early morning had stopped.

84
00:05:26,659 --> 00:05:30,979
The process of life had ceased to be intolerable, he had no longer any impulse

85
00:05:30,979 --> 00:05:35,719
to make faces at the tele screen or shout curses at the top of his voice.

86
00:05:35,719 --> 00:05:40,039
Now that they had a secure Hiding Place almost a home, it did not even seem a

87
00:05:40,039 --> 00:05:44,118
hardship that they could only meet him frequently and for a couple of hours at a time.

88
00:05:44,118 --> 00:05:47,778
What mattered was that the room over the junk shop should exist.

89
00:05:47,778 --> 00:05:52,278
To know that it was there in Violet was almost the same as being in it.

90
00:05:52,278 --> 00:05:57,259
The room was a world, a pocket of the past where extinct animals could walk

91
00:05:57,259 --> 00:06:00,561
to Charrington, thought Winston, was another extinct animal.

92
00:06:00,561 --> 00:06:05,180
He usually stops to talk with Mr Charrington for a few minutes on his way upstairs.

93
00:06:05,180 --> 00:06:09,500
The old man seemed seldom or never to go out of doors, and on the other hand to

94
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:11,840
have almost no customers.

95
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:16,219
He led a ghost-like existence between the tiny, dark shop and an even tinier

96
00:06:16,219 --> 00:06:20,359
back kitchen where he prepared his meals and which contained, among other things,

97
00:06:20,359 --> 00:06:24,379
an unbelievably ancient gramophone with an enormous horn.

98
00:06:24,379 --> 00:06:28,938
He seemed glad of the opportunity to talk wondering about among his worthless

99
00:06:28,938 --> 00:06:32,900
stock, with his long nose and thick spectacles and his bowed shoulders in

100
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:38,298
the velvet jacket, he had always vaguely the heir of being a collector rather than a tradesman.

101
00:06:38,298 --> 00:06:42,979
With a sort of faded enthusiasm he would finger this scrap of rubbish or that— —a

102
00:06:42,979 --> 00:06:47,479
china bottle stopper the painted lid of a broken snuff box a pinch back locket

103
00:06:47,479 --> 00:06:52,458
containing a strand of some long dead baby's hair— —never asking that Winston should buy it,

104
00:06:52,458 --> 00:06:54,379
merely that he should admire it.

105
00:06:54,379 --> 00:06:58,879
To talk to him was like listening to the tinkling of a worn out musical box

106
00:06:58,879 --> 00:07:03,740
He had dragged out from the corners of his memory some more fragments of forgotten rhymes.

107
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:07,458
There was one about Four and Twenty blackbirds, and another about a cow with

108
00:07:07,458 --> 00:07:11,778
a crumpled horn, and another about the death of poor Robin.

109
00:07:11,778 --> 00:07:14,719
'It just occurred to me you might be interested,' he would say with a

110
00:07:14,719 --> 00:07:18,259
deprecating little laugh whenever he produced a new fragment.

111
00:07:18,259 --> 00:07:22,400
But he could never recall more than a few lines of any one rhyme.

112
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:26,539
Both of them knew— —in a way, it was never out of their minds— —that what was now

113
00:07:26,539 --> 00:07:28,640
happening could not last long.

114
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:32,118
There were times when the fact of impending death seemed as palpable as

115
00:07:32,118 --> 00:07:35,479
the bed they lay on, and they would cling together with a sort of despairing

116
00:07:35,479 --> 00:07:40,219
sensuality, like a damned soul grasping at his last morsel of pleasure when the

117
00:07:40,219 --> 00:07:42,979
clock is within five minutes of striking.

118
00:07:42,979 --> 00:07:48,199
But there were also times when they had the illusion not only of safety but of permanence.

119
00:07:48,199 --> 00:07:53,180
So long as they were actually in this room, they both felt, no harm could come to them.

120
00:07:53,180 --> 00:07:57,798
Getting there was difficult and dangerous, but the room itself was sanctuary.

121
00:07:57,798 --> 00:08:01,819
It was as when Winston had gazed into the heart of the paperweight, with the

122
00:08:01,819 --> 00:08:05,719
feeling that it would be possible to get inside that classy world, and that once

123
00:08:05,719 --> 00:08:08,061
inside it time could be arrested.

124
00:08:08,061 --> 00:08:11,240
Often they gave themselves up to daydreams of escape.

125
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:15,080
Their luck would hold indefinitely, and they would carry on their intrigue, just

126
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:18,199
like this, for the remainder of their natural lives.

127
00:08:18,199 --> 00:08:21,919
Or Catherine would die, and by subtle maneuverings Winston and Julia would

128
00:08:21,919 --> 00:08:23,599
succeed in getting married.

129
00:08:23,599 --> 00:08:26,118
Or they would commit suicide together.

130
00:08:26,118 --> 00:08:29,900
Or they would disappear, alter themselves out of recognition, learn to speak with

131
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:35,599
proletarian accents, get jobs in a factory and live out their lives undetected in a Backstreet

132
00:08:35,599 --> 00:08:40,699
It was all nonsense, as they both knew. In reality there was no escape. Even the one

133
00:08:40,699 --> 00:08:45,199
plan that was practicable, suicide, they had no intention of carrying out.

134
00:08:45,199 --> 00:08:49,278
To hang on from day to day and from week to week, spinning out a present that had

135
00:08:49,278 --> 00:08:53,539
no future, seemed an uncomfortable instinct, just as one's lungs will always

136
00:08:53,539 --> 00:08:57,699
draw the next breath so long as there is air available.

137
00:08:57,798 --> 00:09:02,298
Sometimes, too, they talked of engaging in active rebellion against the Party, but

138
00:09:02,298 --> 00:09:05,118
with no notion of how to take the first step.

139
00:09:05,118 --> 00:09:08,840
Even if the fabulous Brotherhood was a reality, there still remained the

140
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:11,298
difficulty of finding one's way into it.

141
00:09:11,298 --> 00:09:15,199
He told her of the strange intimacy that existed, or seemed to exist, between

142
00:09:15,199 --> 00:09:19,520
himself and O'Brien, and of the impulse he sometimes felt, simply to walk into

143
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:24,561
O'Brien's presence announced that he was the enemy of the Party and demand his help.

144
00:09:24,561 --> 00:09:28,938
Curiously enough this did not strike her as an impossibly rash thing to do.

145
00:09:28,938 --> 00:09:32,958
She was used to judging people by their faces, and it seemed natural to her that

146
00:09:32,958 --> 00:09:38,479
Winston should believe O'Brien to be trustworthy on the strength of a single flash of the eyes.

147
00:09:38,479 --> 00:09:42,379
Moreover she took it for granted that everyone, or nearly everyone, secretly

148
00:09:42,379 --> 00:09:47,061
hated the Party and would break the rules if he thought it's safe to do so.

149
00:09:47,061 --> 00:09:51,199
But she refused to believe that widespread organized opposition existed

150
00:09:51,199 --> 00:09:56,240
or could exist. The tales about Goldstein and his underground army, she said, were

151
00:09:56,240 --> 00:10:00,080
simply a lot of rubbish which the Party had invented for its own purposes and

152
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:02,659
which you had to pretend to believe in.

153
00:10:02,659 --> 00:10:07,458
Times beyond number, at Party rallies and spontaneous demonstrations, she had

154
00:10:07,458 --> 00:10:10,699
shouted at the top of her voice for the execution of people whose name she had

155
00:10:10,699 --> 00:10:14,958
never heard and in whose supposed crime she had not the faintest belief.

156
00:10:14,958 --> 00:10:18,798
When public trials were happening she had taken her place in the detachments

157
00:10:18,798 --> 00:10:22,520
from the Youth League who surrounded the courts from morning to night, chanting at

158
00:10:22,520 --> 00:10:25,580
intervals 'Death to the traitors!'

159
00:10:25,580 --> 00:10:29,298
During the Two Minutes Hate she always excelled all others in shouting insults

160
00:10:29,298 --> 00:10:33,740
at Goldstein and she had only the dimmest idea of who Goldstein was and

161
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:36,378
what doctrines he was supposed to represent.

162
00:10:36,378 --> 00:10:39,619
She had grown up since the Revolution and was too young to remember the

163
00:10:39,619 --> 00:10:44,540
ideological battles of the 50s and 60s Such a thing as an independent political

164
00:10:44,540 --> 00:10:49,457
movement was outside her imagination: and in any case the Party was invincible.

165
00:10:49,457 --> 00:10:53,119
It would always exist, and it would always be the same.

166
00:10:53,119 --> 00:10:57,259
You could only rebel against it by secret disobedience or, at most, by

167
00:10:57,259 --> 00:11:02,119
isolated acts of violence such as killing somebody or blowing something up.

168
00:11:02,119 --> 00:11:07,457
In some ways she was far more acute than Winston, and far less susceptible to Party propaganda.

169
00:11:07,457 --> 00:11:11,779
Once when he happened in some connection to mention the war against Eurasia, she

170
00:11:11,779 --> 00:11:15,860
startled him by saying casually that in her opinion the war was not happening.

171
00:11:15,860 --> 00:11:19,040
The rocket bombs which fell daily on London were probably fired by the

172
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:22,819
Government of Oceania itself, 'just to keep people frightened'.

173
00:11:22,819 --> 00:11:27,439
This was an idea that had literally never occurred to him. She also stirred a

174
00:11:27,439 --> 00:11:31,099
sort of envy in him by telling him that during the Two Minutes Hate her greater

175
00:11:31,099 --> 00:11:34,099
difficulty was to avoid bursting out laughing.

176
00:11:34,099 --> 00:11:37,700
But she only questioned the teachings of the Party when they in some way touched

177
00:11:37,700 --> 00:11:42,439
upon her own life. Often she was ready to accept the official mythology, simply

178
00:11:42,439 --> 00:11:46,340
because the difference between truth and falsehood did not seem important to her.

179
00:11:46,340 --> 00:11:51,740
She believed, for instance, having learned it at school, that the Party had invented airplanes

180
00:11:51,740 --> 00:11:55,400
In his own school days Winston remembered, in the late 50s it was only

181
00:11:55,400 --> 00:12:00,560
the helicopter that the Party claimed to have invented; a dozen years later, when Julia was at

182
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:04,400
school, it was already claiming the airplane one generation mourned it would

183
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:06,619
be claiming the steam engine.)

184
00:12:06,619 --> 00:12:10,218
And when he told her that airplanes had been in existence before he was born, and

185
00:12:10,218 --> 00:12:14,599
long before the Revolution, the fact struck her as totally uninteresting.

186
00:12:14,599 --> 00:12:19,040
After all, what did it matter who had invented airplanes It was rather more of

187
00:12:19,040 --> 00:12:22,580
a shock to him when he discovered from some chance remark that she did not

188
00:12:22,580 --> 00:12:28,819
remember that Oceania, four years ago, had been at war with East Asia and at peace with Eurasia.

189
00:12:28,819 --> 00:12:33,080
It was true that she regarded the whole war as a sham: but apparently she had not

190
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:36,020
even noticed that the name of the enemy had changed.

191
00:12:36,020 --> 00:12:39,860
'I thought we'd always been at war with Eurasia,' she said vaguely.

192
00:12:39,860 --> 00:12:44,240
It frightened him a little. The invention of airplanes dated from long before her

193
00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:49,040
birth, but the switchover in the war had happened only four years ago, well after

194
00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:50,779
she was grown up.

195
00:12:50,779 --> 00:12:54,319
He argued with her about it for perhaps a quarter of an hour.

196
00:12:54,319 --> 00:12:58,340
In the end he succeeded in forcing her memory back until she did dimly recall

197
00:12:58,340 --> 00:13:02,718
that at one time East Asia and not Eurasia had been the enemy.

198
00:13:02,718 --> 00:13:06,200
But the issue still struck her as unimportant.

199
00:13:06,200 --> 00:13:10,580
'Who cares?' she said impatiently. 'It's always one bloody war after another,

200
00:13:10,580 --> 00:13:14,540
and one knows the news is all lies anyway.'

201
00:13:14,540 --> 00:13:17,779
Sometimes he talked to her of the Records Department and the impudent

202
00:13:17,779 --> 00:13:19,939
forgeries that he committed there.

203
00:13:19,939 --> 00:13:22,580
Such things did not appear to horrify her.

204
00:13:22,580 --> 00:13:27,797
She did not feel the abyss opening beneath her feet at the thought of lies becoming truths.

205
00:13:27,797 --> 00:13:32,180
He told her the story of Jones, Aaronson and Rutherford and the momentous slip of

206
00:13:32,180 --> 00:13:35,060
paper which he had once held between his fingers.

207
00:13:35,060 --> 00:13:36,860
It did not make much impression on her.

208
00:13:36,860 --> 00:13:41,419
At first, indeed, she failed to grasp the point of the story.

209
00:13:41,419 --> 00:13:44,180
'Were they friends of yours?' she said.

210
00:13:44,180 --> 00:13:48,680
'No, I never knew them. They were Inner Party members. Besides, they were far

211
00:13:48,680 --> 00:13:53,240
older men than I was. They belonged to the old days, before the Revolution.

212
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:58,218
I barely knew them by sight and what was there to worry about? People

213
00:13:58,218 --> 00:14:00,797
are being killed off all the time, aren't they?'

214
00:14:00,797 --> 00:14:03,137
He tried to make her understand.

215
00:14:03,137 --> 00:14:05,180
'This was an exceptional case.

216
00:14:05,180 --> 00:14:09,200
It wasn't just a question of somebody being killed. Do you realize that the

217
00:14:09,200 --> 00:14:12,740
past, starting from yesterday, has been actually abolished?

218
00:14:12,740 --> 00:14:16,340
If it survives anywhere, it's in a few solid objects with no words attached to

219
00:14:16,340 --> 00:14:17,957
them, like that lump of glass there.

220
00:14:17,957 --> 00:14:21,979
Already we know almost literally nothing about the Revolution in the years before

221
00:14:21,979 --> 00:14:23,540
the Revolution.

222
00:14:23,540 --> 00:14:27,378
Every record has been destroyed or falsified, every book has been Rewritten

223
00:14:27,378 --> 00:14:31,457
every picture has been repainted every statue and street and building has been

224
00:14:31,457 --> 00:14:34,099
renamed every date has been altered.

225
00:14:34,099 --> 00:14:38,659
And that process is continuing day by day and minute by minute. History has

226
00:14:38,659 --> 00:14:44,419
stopped. Nothing exists except an endless present in which the Party is always right.

227
00:14:44,419 --> 00:14:48,319
I know, of course, that the past is falsified, but it would never be possible

228
00:14:48,319 --> 00:14:52,580
for me to prove it, even when I did the falsification myself.

229
00:14:52,580 --> 00:14:57,319
After the thing is done, no evidence ever remains. The only evidence is inside my

230
00:14:57,319 --> 00:15:02,718
own mind, and I don't know with any certainty that any other human being shares my memories.

231
00:15:02,718 --> 00:15:06,979
Just in that one instance, in my whole life, I did possess actual concrete

232
00:15:06,979 --> 00:15:10,457
evidence after the event— —years after it.'

233
00:15:10,457 --> 00:15:12,797
'And what good was that?'

234
00:15:12,797 --> 00:15:17,000
'It was no good, because I threw it away a few minutes later. But if the same thing

235
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,700
happened today, I should keep it.'

236
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:23,779
'Well, I wouldn't!' said Julia. 'I'm quite ready to take risks, but only for

237
00:15:23,779 --> 00:15:26,718
something worthwhile not for bits of old newspaper.

238
00:15:26,718 --> 00:15:30,319
What could you have done with it even if you had kept it?'

239
00:15:30,319 --> 00:15:33,259
'Not much, perhaps. But it was evidence.

240
00:15:33,259 --> 00:15:36,439
It might have planted a few doubts here and there, supposing that I'd dared to

241
00:15:36,439 --> 00:15:37,878
show it to anybody.

242
00:15:37,878 --> 00:15:41,840
I don't imagine that we can alter anything in our own lifetime. But one can

243
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:45,619
imagine little knots of resistance spinning up here and there— —small groups

244
00:15:45,619 --> 00:15:49,340
of people banding themselves together, and gradually growing, and even leaving a

245
00:15:49,340 --> 00:15:54,680
few records behind, so that the next Generations can carry on where we leave off.'

246
00:15:54,680 --> 00:15:59,297
'I'm not interested in the next generation, dear. I'm interested in us.'

247
00:15:59,297 --> 00:16:02,599
'You're only a rebel from the waist downwards,' he told her.

248
00:16:02,599 --> 00:16:07,218
She thought this brilliantly witty and flung her arms around him in the light

249
00:16:07,218 --> 00:16:11,659
In the ramifications of Party doctrine she had not the faintest interest.

250
00:16:11,659 --> 00:16:15,259
Whenever he began to talk of the principles of ensoc doublethink, the

251
00:16:15,259 --> 00:16:19,939
mutability of the past and the denial of objective reality, and to use Newspeak

252
00:16:19,939 --> 00:16:23,900
words, she became bored and confused and said that she never paid any attention

253
00:16:23,900 --> 00:16:25,939
to that kind of thing.

254
00:16:25,939 --> 00:16:30,259
One knew that it was all rubbish, so why let oneself be worried by it?

255
00:16:30,259 --> 00:16:34,340
She knew when to cheer and went to boo, and that was all one needed.

256
00:16:34,340 --> 00:16:40,040
If he persisted in talking of such subjects, she had a disconcerting habit of falling asleep.

257
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:44,240
She was one of those people who can go to sleep at any hour and in any position.

258
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:47,540
Talking to her, he realized how easy it was to present an appearance of

259
00:16:47,540 --> 00:16:52,520
orthodoxy while having no grasp whatever of what orthodoxy meant. In a way, the

260
00:16:52,520 --> 00:16:56,360
world view of the Party imposed itself most successfully on people incapable of

261
00:16:56,360 --> 00:16:58,099
understanding it.

262
00:16:58,099 --> 00:17:01,939
They could be made to accept the most flagrant violations of reality, because

263
00:17:01,939 --> 00:17:06,137
they never fully grasped the enormity of what was demanded of them, and were not

264
00:17:06,137 --> 00:17:10,099
sufficiently interested in public events to notice what was happening.

265
00:17:10,099 --> 00:17:13,040
By lack of understanding they remains sane.

266
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:16,637
They simply swallowed everything, and what they swallowed did them no harm,

267
00:17:16,637 --> 00:17:21,439
because it left no residue behind, just as a grain of corn will pass undigested

268
00:17:21,439 --> 00:17:23,000
through the body of a bird
